<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Riograndense Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1836 CE – 1845 CE · capital Piratini</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A breakaway republic in southern Brazil born of the Ragamuffin War, which fought the empire for nearly a decade and gave Garibaldi his first battles.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In September 1835 ranchers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a>, angry at imperial taxes that favoured imported jerked beef, rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bento-goncalves">Bento Gonçalves</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ragamuffin-war">Ragamuffin War</a>, or Farroupilha. After victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-seival">Seival</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-sousa-neto">Antônio de Sousa Neto</a> proclaimed the Riograndense Republic on 11 September 1836.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bento-goncalves">Bento Gonçalves</a> served as president, and the republic's capital moved between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piratini">Piratini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacapava-do-sul">Caçapava</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alegrete">Alegrete</a> as fighting shifted. It was run by a ranching elite and recruited gauchos and enslaved black soldiers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanceiros-negros">Lanceiros Negros</a>, promising them freedom. The Italian revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> fought for it, leading an expedition that created the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/juliana-republic">Juliana Republic</a> in 1839.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The imperial commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-alves-de-lima-e-silva-duke-of-caxias">Luís Alves de Lima e Silva</a>, later Duke of Caxias, combined military pressure with negotiation, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ponche-verde">Treaty of Ponche Verde</a> on 1 March 1845 ended the war with an amnesty. The republic's flag inspired the flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/riograndense-republic.jpg" alt="Riograndense Republic" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Original: Tito Livio Zambeccari Vector: LipeFontoura · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Piratini_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1836 CE – 1845 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Piratini</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bento Gonçalves, Antônio de Sousa Neto, Davi Canabarro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/brazil">Brazil</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of the Rio Grande do Sul, Piratini Republic, República Rio-Grandense</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=riograndense-republic" aria-label="Riograndense Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3310 3323 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Riograndense Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3630 3579L3846 3279M3630 3579L3724 3476"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3846 3279h0M3724 3476h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3630 3579h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3630 3579h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Piratini. <a href="/atlas?year=1840&amp;focus=riograndense-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
